1783543599871075	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExigE		item.update	bn-13s	{"field":"title","value":"Formal model of commit/recovery protocol (Stateright primary; TLA+ independence check optional)"}	blake3:cnOsVQwhaRqxZCUcT7-1P4z1YlGWcMoQB5leFWFx3vM
1783543599876310	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExiwE		item.update	bn-13s	{"field":"description","value":"# Context & Background\nThe torn-write harness empirically checks the commit protocol against ONE fault model\n(independent sector persistence, 24k randomized cases). A model checker verifies the\nprotocol's LOGIC against ALL behaviors a reordering-disk abstraction permits within bounds —\nprotocol-level races the harness's specific fault model can't represent. This is the sweet\nspot between \"fold associativity is trivially true\" and \"Perennial-scale proof effort.\"\n\n# Approach (decided: Stateright as primary — see reasoning)\nModel in Rust with the stateright crate: writer/committer steps, a reordering disk (pending\nwrites persist as arbitrary subsets between barriers), crash, and recovery. THE KEY MOVE:\nthe model's recovery transition calls the PRODUCTION batch-acceptance predicate (the pure\nA1/A9/A10 function from mess-log) as its guard — the checker exhaustively verifies the real\ndecision logic, not a transcription of it. Finitize small (2-4 batches, a handful of sectors);\nexhaustive BFS as a cargo test in CI.\n\nProperties: acked-implies-recovered, no-partial-batch-visible, recovery idempotence,\nno-stale-generation acceptance.\n\n# Reasoning / alternatives considered\nStateright over TLA+ as primary because: (1) it checks the actual Rust predicate — kills\ntranscription drift, the classic external-spec failure mode for a small team; (2) it runs as\na cargo test next to the other harnesses (the pile's philosophy: every correctness claim is\nexecutable CI); (3) team fluency. What we give up: independence (a Rust model can share the\nimplementation's blind spots) and Apalache's symbolic unbounded-int checking — acceptable\nbecause protocol models get finitized anyway, and independence is recoverable via the\noptional TLA+ pass below. Lean 4 demoted to a footnote: proofs of the trivial algebra add\nelegance not assurance; protocol-level proofs are research-scale.\n\n# Optional independence pass\nA small TLA+/Apalache spec of the same protocol as a cross-check — worth doing if the\nStateright model finds anything subtle, or before publishing crash-safety claims. Toolchain\nis already pinned and verified in mise.toml: `mise run fetch-formal`, `mise run tlc -- ...`,\n`mise run apalache -- check ...` (JDK 17 + tla2tools 1.8.0 + Apalache 0.58.2; Lean 4.31.0\nalso available via mise if the mood strikes).\n\n# Synergy\nThe Stateright model's state/oracle types should be shared with the DST harness's in-memory\nreference model (bn-3kn) — the model exhaustively checked at small scale is the same oracle\nthe simulator checks against at large scale.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Stateright model covers the D2 protocol incl. A1/A9/A10, with the production\n      acceptance predicate called directly as the transition guard\n- [ ] Exhaustive check green within stated bounds, running as a cargo test in CI\n- [ ] Any counterexample becomes a spec fix + a concrete harness case\n- [ ] Findings (or clean bill within bounds) recorded as a note in docs/spec/"}	blake3:NqBWl7nHOyK19RHy04A5Ry6nXfOZsta7f92QhlNCheI
1783543777266447	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExjAE		item.create	bn-2za	{"description":"# Context & Background\nThe store DETECTS at-rest corruption precisely (per-batch CRC localizes damage to exact\nblocks; fold chains prove content) but cannot REPAIR it — today's answer is backup restore.\nA seal-time parity sidecar closes that gap for latent sector errors / bit rot on sealed\nsegments: mess verify --repair reconstructs damaged blocks offline.\n\n# Approach\nReed-Solomon, NOT RaptorQ/fountain codes: for static local parity with known redundancy,\nRS is MDS-optimal and deterministic (reed-solomon-simd crate is fast at these sizes);\nrateless codes' advantages (unknown loss rates, many receivers, no feedback) don't apply\non-device — their future home is replication catch-up / anti-entropy transport, distribution\nera (note kept here so the idea isn't lost).\nShape: at seal, RS(k=~64 data blocks, m=2-4 parity) per group into a sidecar file referenced\nfrom the segment footer (the footer spec RESERVES this optional reference — see bn-21c\nacceptance criteria — so this is never a format break). Repair flow: CRC localizes bad\nblocks -> parity reconstructs -> batch CRC + fold certificate re-verify PROVES the repair\nrestored the exact committed bytes. Detect -> repair -> cryptographically verified, offline.\n\n# Reasoning / why post-v1 and evidence-gated\nSame-device parity does not survive device death (backups/replication remain the real\ndurability story); SSDs already run internal LDPC; whole-device failure dominates observed\nSSD failure modes. ~3-6% storage + trivial seal CPU is cheap, but the design discipline says\nfeatures enter by measured need — adopt if verify telemetry or user demand shows latent\ncorruption actually occurring, or as a differentiating ops feature when the CLI matures.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Sidecar built at seal behind a config flag (default off until evidence)\n- [ ] verify --repair reconstructs deliberately-corrupted blocks (harness corruptors reused)\n      and re-verifies via CRC + fold certificate before declaring success\n- [ ] Storage overhead and seal-time cost measured and recorded in the ledger\n- [ ] Decision note: adopt-by-default or keep opt-in, with evidence","kind":"task","labels":["ops","research"],"parent":"bn-2sk","size":"m","title":"Seal-time RS parity sidecar + verify --repair (evidence-gated)"}	blake3:7TBT0RPWGyNyaDxWLswfsThV3kxc5spUQR2T5-5-fZU
1783543794551454	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExjQE		item.link	bn-2za	{"link_type":"blocks","target":"bn-1l6"}	blake3:09MTgFOalqNUer9roLlre-tJb6nqKVfsypdjjlbCatw
1783543794569542	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExjgE		item.link	bn-2za	{"link_type":"blocks","target":"bn-eyy"}	blake3:CM8tdYPmXVm153o-Bm25Y1aVOJOfv21DdnLHTN33xmY
1783543809729031	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExjwE		item.update	bn-21c	{"field":"description","value":"# Context\nThe normative core: log-with-commit-markers as sole authority, batch framing, all twelve\nacceptance rules, recovery rules, 256 MiB segments. Everything exists in doc 12 amendment\nblocks + spike code (crash_log format v1, vertical_slice format v2, torn_write) but there\nare now TWO dialects of the format in spike code — the spec must pick one normative layout.\n\n# Approach\ndocs/spec/01-log-format.md + 02-recovery.md. Byte-layout tables for BatchHeader /\nEventSubframe (incl. schema_version u16, dict_id u16, codec_id) / CommitMarker; CRC split\ncoverage (R4 — hash AROUND checksum fields, never copy-and-zero); segment header/footer\nincl. A9 epoch (R3); recovery algorithm as pseudocode with the A-rules inline where they\nbite; parallel-scan allowance (R1) and manifest footer cache (R2).\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Every A1-A12 and R1-R4 rule appears exactly once, normatively\n- [ ] A clean-room implementer could produce byte-identical output from the tables alone\n- [ ] Torn-write fault model documented as the conformance bar (spikes/torn_write = 24k cases)\n- [ ] Segment footer reserves an OPTIONAL repair-sidecar reference (forward-compat for the\n      RS parity sidecar, bn-2za) so adding at-rest repair later is never a format break"}	blake3:ktYaWs3LULp_xDSW0l4F-if5NN4VMoB6EpPvaMhTQ94
1783544266429645	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExkAE		item.create	bn-36y	{"description":"# Context\nDisk-full is the classic storage-engine humiliation and nothing in the design accounts\nfor it: ENOSPC can strike mid-batch write, mid-fdatasync (fsyncgate's ugly cousin —\nthe kernel may report it at fsync time), mid-seal, or inside fjall. Unhandled, any of\nthese can wedge or corrupt; handled naively (retry), they violate the D8 discipline.\n\n# Approach\n(1) PREALLOCATE segments at roll time (fallocate the full 256 MiB) so allocation failure\nhappens at ONE predictable, recoverable point — segment roll — instead of mid-commit;\na failed roll fails the triggering append with a typed StoreFull error, store stays\nconsistent and readable. (2) ENOSPC during fsync or metadata writes follows the D8\npoisoning path (state unknowable -> poison writes, degraded reads, restart+recovery).\n(3) Seal failures on ENOSPC retry later — sealing is deferrable, never lossy.\n(4) Fault harness gains ENOSPC injection points at every write/fsync/fallocate site\n(the Fs trait from bn-z98 makes this trivial to inject).\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] fallocate-at-roll implemented; roll failure -> typed StoreFull, no partial segment\n- [ ] Harness: ENOSPC injected at each site class; store never corrupts, recovery always\n      succeeds once space returns\n- [ ] Documented operator guidance (mess doctor reports headroom; metrics expose it)\n","kind":"task","labels":["storage","harness"],"parent":"bn-2lq","size":"s","title":"ENOSPC policy: segment preallocation + disk-full injection cases"}	blake3:nghJ9a2K4VSLUsJwpTlDwj_T0pg0Rk74WPOSttvTIpA
1783544266538733	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExkQE		item.create	bn-1gx	{"description":"# Context\nThe verification stack has Kani (pure functions, all inputs), DST (task-level\ninterleavings), and fuzzing (hostile bytes) — but nothing exercises MEMORY-ORDERING\ninterleavings of the cross-thread atomics. Three protocols carry real ordering burden:\nthe durable watermark publish (D11's gapless invariant IS watermark-advance-before-\npublish — a relaxed-ordering bug here is undetectable by the other layers), the\ncommitter group handoff, and the active-index swap at seal.\n\n# Approach\nloom tests over small extracted versions of each protocol (loom needs the sync\nprimitives behind its types — factor the protocols so the atomics are swappable, which\nthe bn-z98 abstraction work already encourages). Keep models tiny (2-3 threads, 2-3\noperations) — loom's exhaustiveness explodes fast. Run in CI (loom tests are normal\ncargo tests under cfg(loom)).\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Watermark publish protocol passes loom exhaustively; a deliberately-relaxed\n      ordering (Release->Relaxed) FAILS the loom test (test of the test)\n- [ ] Committer handoff and seal-time index swap modeled and green\n- [ ] cfg(loom) lane wired into CI\n","kind":"task","labels":["storage","verification"],"parent":"bn-2lq","size":"s","title":"Loom tests: watermark publish, committer handoff, index swap"}	blake3:VJwh2oAnG6uqZuLGCVrkAwegeQ1sHW-mYarIOkFzhTo
1783544266653019	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExkgE		item.create	bn-25j	{"description":"# Context\nThe read path is mmap + zero-copy decode — exactly where UB hides in otherwise-safe\nRust. Miri catches UB in the pure decode/encode units; ASAN/TSAN catch what Miri can't\nreach (I/O-touching integration paths, real threading races).\n\n# Approach\nMiri lane over the unit-level encode/decode/codec tests (Miri can't do real I/O — scope\naccordingly, keep a miri-compatible test subset marked). ASAN + TSAN nightly lane over\nthe harness fast profiles. Both are CI configuration + a handful of test-organization\ntweaks, not new test content.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] cargo miri test green on the decode/codec subset, wired into CI\n- [ ] ASAN and TSAN nightly lanes green over harness fast profiles\n- [ ] A planted use-after-free in a scratch branch is caught (sanity-check the lanes)\n","kind":"task","labels":["verification","infra"],"parent":"bn-2lq","size":"s","title":"Miri + sanitizer CI lanes (decode paths, harnesses)"}	blake3:VAGBn5nV9G4IT1zuZiMPKEvYrrQ1AF_YY23vYWDGEJU
1783544266766675	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExkwE		item.create	bn-2ln	{"description":"# Context & Background\nBackups ARE the durability story for a single-node store (device death is the dominant\nfailure mode; we rejected same-device parity partly on this basis — bn-2za). Yet\n\"consistent online backup\" is undefined: what is a correct cut while the writer is live?\nUndefined-but-easy is how people lose data.\n\n# Why the design makes this unusually clean\nSealed segments are immutable (copy-safe live, rsync/hardlink-friendly, content-stable\nso incremental = new segments only). The log is the sole authority (D1) and every index\nis rebuildable (I5), so a backup needs only: sealed segments + the active segment up to\nthe durable watermark + the manifest. A torn tail beyond the watermark is HARMLESS —\nrestore runs recovery, and recovery truncates it by design. Restore verification is\ntherefore recovery + verify, machinery that already exists and is harness-proven.\n\n# Approach\n`mess backup <dest>`: (1) take a retention LEASE so segment deletion cannot race the\ncopy (extends doc 05's blocker list; `mess retention explain` shows it); (2) record a\nmanifest snapshot + the durable watermark as the cut; (3) copy sealed segments\n(hardlink locally / rsync remotely); (4) copy the active segment prefix up to the cut;\n(5) write a backup manifest with per-file checksums. Metadata (fjall) optionally\nincluded as a restore-speed optimization, never a correctness requirement.\nIncremental: skip sealed segments already present at dest (by name+checksum).\n`mess restore` = copy back + recovery + verify --full.\nALSO: document the no-CLI path (plain rsync ordering rules) — the \"boring operation\"\npromise includes people with existing backup tooling.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Backup taken DURING active writing restores to a store that passes verify --full\n      and contains every event acked before the cut (differential replay checksums)\n- [ ] Writer crashed mid-backup: backup still restores cleanly (cut semantics hold)\n- [ ] Incremental backup copies only new sealed segments (measured)\n- [ ] Retention lease test: deletion blocked mid-backup, released after\n- [ ] Documented rsync-only procedure with ordering rules\n","kind":"task","labels":["ops","dx"],"parent":"bn-2sk","size":"m","title":"Online backup + restore: consistent cut, incremental, retention lease"}	blake3:sDbNOUxeMwZKSDjbHW_Eo__MiyR5sjrt2LCAPD_hlg4
